{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261110T000725Z-74106453", "ts": "2026-11-10T00:15:08Z", "actor": {"worktree": "cordyceps", "plugin": "3.8.1-dev", "branch": "fix/strict-inputs", "build": "52bb14851a37"}, "body": {"base_commit": "53ea43b8ef95e2b2c8a98fde2b721a8e5a53ddd1", "base_tree": "35d46509dac713f67d1b52f7febf4cec9cc6a158", "head_tree": "07232d67501fef8ff71b82d557189f8a510ddbbd", "head_commit": "0187b7e8ec77c37c7b0b7c51002230f45bf49165", "dispatch_commit": "0187b7e8ec77c37c7b0b7c51002230f45bf49165", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}], "files_reviewed": ["CHANGELOG.md", "LICENSE", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/InfrastructureIdsTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ParamSpecLookupTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ToolArgumentBinderTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TypeNameRuleTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/InfrastructureIds.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/NameSuggestions.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ParamSpecLookup.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolArgumentBinder.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/ComponentPatternsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Patterns/GridArray.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/CreateParametricGeometry.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/PlanDefinition.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Resources/ResourceRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md", ".prawduct/changes/strict-inputs.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "LICENSE", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/InfrastructureIdsTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ParamSpecLookupTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ToolArgumentBinderTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TypeNameRuleTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/InfrastructureIds.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/NameSuggestions.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ParamSpecLookup.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolArgumentBinder.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/ComponentPatternsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Patterns/GridArray.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/CreateParametricGeometry.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/PlanDefinition.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Resources/ResourceRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md", ".prawduct/changes/strict-inputs.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint: four declared chunk deliverables do not resolve to files", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (four lines of the build plan). Relayed from the manifest's record_lint, check chunk-ref-missing: chunk 01 line 63 `Core/InfrastructureIds.cs`; chunk 02 line 69 `tools/call`; chunk 03 line 78 `Category/Name`; chunk 03 line 79 `Category/Subcategory/Name`. Why it broke: the plan backticks slash-bearing tokens in deliverable lines, and the check reads each as a repo path. For the builder's benefit, what the tree shows: the chunk 01 file exists at src/Cordyceps/Core/InfrastructureIds.cs (the plan wrote it relative to the plugin project, not the repo root), and the other three are a protocol method name and two name spellings, not files; the code they describe is present (Core/ToolArgumentBinder.cs, Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs). The remedy is in the plan text, not the code: write the chunk 01 deliverable as its repo-root path and take the backticks off (or rephrase) the three non-path tokens so the deliverable check passes on re-run.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'Python 3 Script' pin selects the unified Script component, a different type from the one named", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one entry of TypeNameRule.Pinned), with the other four pins worth the same check. TypeNameRule.Pinned maps 'Python 3 Script' to c9b2d725-6f87-4b07-af90-bd9aefef68eb. This repo's own docs/upstream-rhino-scriptcomponent-languagespec.md identifies that GUID as the unified 'Script' component (RhinoCodePluginGH.Components.ScriptComponent), which has no language until one is chosen. So type='Python 3 Script' and the aliases python / 'python script' / ghpython resolve by pin to a type with a different name, and the pin runs before the exact-name step that would otherwise find the dedicated component. That is the defect this plan exists to remove (a call acts on something other than what it named), and the new help and changelog text say the opposite: \"'Python 3 Script' ... always mean the built-in Grasshopper type\". Consequence: an agent asking for a Python 3 Script gets a language-less Script component and meets the 'Can not determine input code language' path that CommonErrorsGuide says the dedicated component avoids. The mapping was carried over from the old KnownGuids table, so behaviour is unchanged from before; it is rated here because this change re-homes it as a rule and documents it as exact. Why the suite cannot see it: the TypeNameRuleTests catalog gives that GUID the name 'Python 3 Script', so the alias test passes on a fixture that does not match an install. Also note the failure mode differs from what VRF-018 item 5 describes: a pin whose GUID is installed as a different type does not fall back to the ordinary rule, it silently creates that other type. Fix by one of: drop the pin so the exact-name rule resolves 'Python 3 Script'; pin the dedicated component's GUID once confirmed in a live session; or, if the unified Script component is the intended target, stop presenting it as 'Python 3 Script' in the pin table, tool help and changelog. Whichever is chosen, make the test fixture name that GUID as an install does. I could not confirm the dedicated component's GUID from the tree.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TypeNameRuleTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Help advertises shorthands that land on names the same help calls ambiguous", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded by the TypeNameRule.Aliases table. An alias rewrites to a bare name and then goes through the ordinary rule, so an alias whose target is shared by a component and a parameter can never resolve. The gh_canvas add tip lists 'ln' as a working shorthand while the next tip says plain 'Line' is ambiguous; 'pt' is listed in that tip, in GettingStartedGuide and in GetServerInstructions while ComponentPatternsGuide lists Point among the names shared with a parameter; 'circ' and 'rect' target Circle and Rectangle, which the guides also call shared (the test Resolve_AnAliasThatLandsOnAnAmbiguousName_IsAmbiguousToo pins 'circ' as ambiguous). Consequence: an agent that follows the documented shorthand list gets ambiguous_name and spends a turn, against the chunk 03 done-criterion that every documented example still works. Why it broke: the alias table was carried over unchanged while the documentation around it was rewritten for the strict rule. Remedy by construction rather than by editing the list: give each such alias a qualified target (the rewrite happens before the Category/Name parse, so 'ln' -> 'Curve/Line' resolves) or remove it, then document only aliases that resolve. Which type each shorthand should mean (component or parameter) is an owner decision. To search: every Aliases value, checked against the names the guides list as shared.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "No evidence the change was exercised beyond unit tests; every add now runs through host code that has not executed", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. VRF-016, VRF-017 and VRF-018 are all pending, and the plan records the Grasshopper-side adapters as unverified until a live session. This change carries more host risk than a typical one: gh_canvas add, gh_inspect docs and gh://component now all pass through ComponentRegistry.BuildCatalog (proxy.Type, FindAssembly per library) on every call, Panel and Number Slider are created through EmitObject on a pinned GUID where they used to be constructed directly, and strict matching makes every name in the guides load-bearing where loose matching used to rescue a near miss. Consequence: a fault in the catalog adapter on a real install fails every add, and the unit suite cannot show it. Run the three entries before the release that carries this, or record the accept. Two gaps in the queue worth closing when it is run: item 1 does not exercise the other advertised shorthands (plane, pt, crv, num, int, bool, str), and item 9 checks Rectangular Grid's port names but not that the component name used in Patterns/GridArray.md resolves as a type (my recollection of the stock library is that the component is named 'Rectangular'; that is from memory, not from this tree, so treat it as a thing to check).", "files": [".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Patterns/GridArray.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Advertised type shorthands rewrite to names the same help calls ambiguous", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the alias table moved verbatim from ComponentRegistry into TypeNameRule.Aliases, but an alias is now a rewrite that goes through the strict rule, so an alias whose target is a shared name can only ever return ambiguous_name. TypeNameRule.Aliases maps circ -> Circle, ln -> Line and rect -> Rectangle; the gh_canvas add Tips say in one line that 'Circle' and 'Line' exist as both a component and a parameter and are ambiguous, and in another list 'ln' among the working shorthands. ComponentPatternsGuide.md adds Rectangle and Point to the shared-name list, while 'pt' (-> Point) is advertised as a shorthand in the Tips, GetServerInstructions() and GettingStartedGuide.md; one of those two statements about Point is wrong. The test Resolve_AnAliasThatLandsOnAnAmbiguousName_IsAmbiguousToo pins the mechanism for 'circ', so the rule is behaving as designed; what is incoherent is shipping and advertising shorthands that cannot resolve. Consequence: an agent that reads action='help' and passes type='ln' gets a refusal for a spelling the help told it to use, and the kept-spellings list the plan says the coordinator confirms and shows the owner contains entries with no working behaviour. Remedy: for every alias, decide what it means (a qualified target such as 'Curve/Line' or 'Params/Line' resolves through the same rule) or delete it; then advertise only shorthands whose targets are unqualified-unique or qualified. To bound the class, check each value in TypeNameRule.Aliases against the names the guides list as shared (Circle, Line, Arc, Rectangle, Point) and add the advertised shorthands (pt, crv, ln, num, int, bool, str, cube, cyl, rect, circ) to the VRF-018 live check, which today exercises only slider, python, c#, csharp Script and filter. I could not confirm a real install's catalog from source; the ambiguity of Circle, Line and Rectangle is taken from this change's own documentation.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/ComponentPatternsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CLAUDE.md still describes ComponentRegistry as the place names and aliases are resolved", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the change moved the alias table and every matching rule out of ComponentRegistry and the Architecture section of CLAUDE.md was not part of the documentation audit. CLAUDE.md line 38 reads 'Core/ComponentRegistry.cs - Resolves component type names to actual Grasshopper components. Handles aliases (e.g., \"python\" -> \"Python 3 Script\") and supports creation by name or GUID.' After this change ComponentRegistry's own summary says it 'carries no matching rule of its own': aliases, pins and the resolution rule live in Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs, and the new rule homes (Core/Naming/, Core/ToolArgumentBinder.cs, Core/InfrastructureIds.cs, Core/ParamSpecLookup.cs) are not mentioned. Consequence: CLAUDE.md is loaded into every session, so the follow-on waves the plan says will share these rules start from a description that sends them to the adapter to add an alias or change resolution. Remedy: replace the ComponentRegistry entry with one that says it is the Grasshopper adapter (catalog, creation, search) and names Core/Naming/TypeNameRule as the single type rule. Replace rather than append: CLAUDE.md is already 217 lines, about 190 of them outside the PRAWDUCT anchor.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Search labels 'deprecated' by a narrower rule than the one that resolves names", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to three sites, all in src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs (search for 'Obsolete ||'). Why it broke: the change built one catalog for resolution and left SearchComponents reading the proxies itself. BuildCatalog marks a type deprecated when proxy.Obsolete, DeprecationRegistry.IsDeprecated, or the type name carries OBSOLETE; both branches of SearchComponents use only the first two, and repeat the same twelve-line ComponentInfo projection twice. Consequence: gh_canvas(action='search') can report deprecated=false for a type that add treats as deprecated. Every refusal now sends the agent to search to pick a candidate, so an agent can copy the GUID of an OBSOLETE-named type that search called current, and add then creates it without the deprecated warning path being reachable by GUID. A later change to deprecation detection also has to be made in two places with nothing tying them together. Remedy: make the catalog the one owner. SearchComponents becomes a filter and ordering over BuildCatalog().All (TypeCandidate already carries Description, UpgradeTo and UpgradeToGuid), which retires the duplicated projection and lets ComponentInfo go, since it is TypeCandidate minus Plugin with the GUID as a string. Search keeps its substring matching; only the source of the rows changes. If search's 'deprecated' field changes for some types as a result, that is a user-visible change for the CHANGELOG.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "TryCreate's tuple can say 'created' and 'GuidNotInstalled' at once", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the GUID fallback and the instantiation failure were added as extra tuple combinations rather than as resolution kinds. ComponentRegistry.TryCreate returns (component, resolution, error) and four combinations are reachable: (component, Resolved, null); (component, GuidNotInstalled, null) when EmitObject succeeds for a GUID no listed proxy carries; (null, unresolved, Explain text); and (null, Resolved, 'could not be instantiated'). Two signals, one call site today (GhCanvasTool add), which handles them correctly by testing component == null first and resolution.IsResolved twice afterwards. The fourth combination is also why the host-free rule's CodeFor maps Resolved to 'not_instantiated', an adapter outcome named inside the rule. Borderline and localized, so a note: if the object-name resolver or batch editing in the follow-on waves becomes a second caller, give the outcome one owner (for example a small result type whose kinds include EmittedByGuid and NotInstantiated) rather than have each caller re-derive it from the tuple.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "ToolArgumentBinder carries its own edit distance beside NameSuggestions", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: chunks 02 and 03 were built in separate worktrees and each wrote the helper it needed. ToolArgumentBinder.Distance is the same two-row Levenshtein as NameSuggestions.EditDistance, and ToolArgumentBinder.Suggest is a third 'closest name' budget (1 edit under five characters, else 2) next to NameSuggestions.Closest (max(2, length/3)) and TypeNameRule.NearMisses (max(2, length/4)). Nothing misbehaves, and the differing budgets may be deliberate, so this is a note. The plan names Core/Naming as the neutral home the later waves share; the cheap alignment is to delete ToolArgumentBinder.Distance and call NameSuggestions.EditDistance (both files are already linked into Cordyceps.Tests), keeping the binder's own budget so its tests are unaffected.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolArgumentBinder.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/NameSuggestions.cs"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "McpServer.ConvertJsonValue has no caller left", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the binding loop that was its only caller moved to Core.ToolArgumentBinder, which calls JsonTypeConverter.ConvertJsonValue directly. The private static forwarder at src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs line 932 is now dead (no other reference in src, and McpServer is not a partial class). Delete the line.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Common-errors row quotes 'Unknown arguments:' for an error that is singular when one name is wrong", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the guide row was written from the plural form only. ToolArgumentBinder emits 'Unknown argument: ...' for one unknown name and 'Unknown arguments: ...' for several; the Misc row in CommonErrorsGuide.md quotes only the plural. An agent holding the real error text is not misled, so this is prose and a note. If the row is touched, key it on the part that does not vary ('Unknown argument') rather than on one of the two forms. The same literal appears in the VRF-017 live check for a one-argument call, where an operator comparing strings would see 'argument' against an expected 'arguments'.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Instantiation-failure error sends the caller to a log that nothing writes", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `ComponentRegistry.TryCreate` returns \"'<path>' (<guid>) resolved but could not be instantiated; see gh_inspect(action='log').\" when `CreateByGuid` (`EmitObject`) returns null for a resolved type, but no code on that path logs: `CreateByGuid` is a bare `EmitObject(...) as IGH_DocumentObject`, `TryCreate` returns the tuple, and `GhCanvasTool.ActionAdd` serialises `ErrorPayload` and returns (its only log line is the Info entry written before resolution). It broke because the message was written as if the pre-change `DebugLog` calls around creation still existed; they were removed with `CreateByName`. Consequence: an agent that hits a plugin type which fails to emit follows the instruction, spends a call on `gh_inspect(action='log')`, and finds nothing about the failure; a maintainer has no record of which GUID failed. Fix at the one site: `ComponentRegistry` is host-coupled (not linked into the test project), so it can call `Core.DebugLog.Error` with the path and GUID at the null return in `TryCreate` - or drop the pointer from the message if nothing more than the message is known.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ComponentRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "scope-trace: shorthands circ, ln and rect can only reach an ambiguity error, and the add help advertises ln", "recommendation": "Scope: class. `TypeNameRule.Aliases` rewrites `circ` -> `Circle`, `ln` -> `Line`, `rect` -> `Rectangle`, and the rewritten name then goes through the ordinary rule - where this same change documents those names as shared by a component and a parameter (CHANGELOG: \"Plain `Circle`/`Line` are now ambiguous\"; ComponentPatternsGuide: \"Circle, Line, Arc, Rectangle, Point\"). `Resolve_AnAliasThatLandsOnAnAmbiguousName_IsAmbiguousToo` pins the outcome for `circ`, so the result is known, but no record says why a shorthand whose only reachable result on a stock install is `ambiguous_name` is kept, and the `add` tips in `GhCanvasTool` list `ln` among the working shorthands. It broke because the alias table was carried over verbatim from the old registry while the names it points at were being declared ambiguous. Consequence: an agent that reads the help and passes `type='ln'` spends a turn on an error for a spelling the help told it to use. The class is every `Aliases` entry whose target has more than one current type in a stock catalog - check all 28 entries, `pt` -> `Point` included, since the guide lists Point as shared; expect the answer to need the live install VRF-018 already queues. It closes by construction, not by editing the list: point each alias at a spelling that resolves alone (alias targets already pass through `SplitQualified`, so `Curve/Line` works as a target) and add a test that iterates `Aliases` against a catalog modelling the shared stock names and asserts each one resolves; or delete the aliases that cannot resolve and take `ln` out of the tip.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Naming/TypeNameRule.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TypeNameRuleTests.cs"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions in strict-inputs, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none - the mandated list, no defect. (1) Strict resolution ships as a breaking change with no lenient mode: settled by the owner on 2026-11-09, recorded in the plan's Goals and the change record. (2) The two `ParamSpecLookupTests` that pinned substring matching were rewritten to the strict contract rather than deleted: owner decision 1, recorded in the plan and the change record; the diff matches (two tests rewritten, none removed). (3) Search and find keep loose matching because they return candidates and select nothing: plan Context, change record, and the `SearchComponents` doc comment. (4) Error code moves from `error` to `code`, and the type catalog is rebuilt per call: both settled by the builder, both in the change record's Decisions with a reason. (5) Settled by the delegate and recorded only in CHANGELOG, tests and code comments, not in the change record's Decisions: a deprecated type is used when it is the sole match (the plan's rule statement says \"among non-obsolete components\"), the pinned built-ins grew from three to five (Panel and Number Slider replace direct construction), `Name (Category)` is kept as a fourth qualified form, and argument names are matched case-sensitively. (6) The owner's norm stated mid-build - new capabilities arrive as actions on the existing tools, batch editing is not an eighth tool - lives only in this plan; the `project-preferences.md` row is promised for \"when the first wave that adds an action is planned\", nothing triggers that, and the \"handoff notes\" the plan cites for the follow-on program are not in the repository. (7) Alignment-pass miss: Requirements Confidence for chunk 03 rests on the coordinator confirming the delegate's keep-versus-remove spelling list and showing it to the owner in the PR; no record carries that list yet, so the PR body is where it is owed. (8) Still open: the release number for two `Breaking:` entries - `docs/release-process.md` says semver with a +0.0.1 patch as the default.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md", ".prawduct/changes/strict-inputs.md"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: the plan repeats the chunk-ref pattern a core rule already names", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The rule \"Build-plan chunk headings must be `### Chunk NN: title`, refs repo-root-relative\" says to write `src/Cordyceps/Core/Foo.cs`, not `Core/Foo.cs`, and to avoid backticked slash-containing non-paths; it records two earlier recurrences. `build-plan-strict-inputs.md` does both again: `Core/InfrastructureIds.cs` at line 63 (the file exists at `src/Cordyceps/Core/InfrastructureIds.cs`), and `tools/call`, `Category/Name`, `Category/Subcategory/Name` at lines 69 and 78-79. These are the four `chunk-ref-missing` entries in the manifest's `record_lint`; that relay carries the severity and this note adds none - it records that the rule fired a third time. A check owns the class, so the remedy is the four edits: root-relative path on line 63, and unbacktick or split the three non-paths.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "rule-unenforced: commit the build plan before dispatching worktree delegates", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The core rule \"Give worktree subagents the WHY behind a constraint, not just the constraint\" also requires: \"commit the build plan before dispatching, since worktree-isolated agents read HEAD\". In this range the plan commit `ce52416` (16:48:37) is a sibling of all three delegate commits, not an ancestor: `55a5c28`, `0aa2f97` and `75fb34b` each have `53ea43b` as parent, and chunk 01's commit (16:46:58) predates the plan commit. No delegate could have read the plan from its worktree. Nothing visibly broke - history is fixed and no edit is wanted - but nothing checks this rule either: `record_lint`'s checks and the hooks read the plan's content, never its position in history. Mechanizing it takes one ancestry test at integration: for each merge in the reviewed range whose second parent is a delegate head, require `git merge-base --is-ancestor <commit that introduced the scope's build plan> <delegate head>`, reported the way `chunk-ref-missing` is.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-strict-inputs.md"], "fid": "R-16"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: #88, #89 and #90 appear resolved", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/cordyceps#88 \"infrastructure: only the first Cordyceps component is protected\" (`Core/InfrastructureIds` plus the `GetCordycepsInfrastructureIds` adapter now cover every bridge). Backlog item appears resolved: #